#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167860} :activity/id #uuid "0a53a252-6ac4-561a-b529-a6d8a0a14ceb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050967 :topic/id #uuid "764fe6dd-703b-527f-a798-104e24a81888" :topic/math-academy-id 3240} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168023 :tutorial/content "In this topic, we'll study phase portraits of linear systems\n![](images/6f/6f6a92e2b8fb89f833268a5526876cb3af5215db0193a2aaaad8aac5790752ea.svg)\nwhere the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has a *repeated* real eigenvalue $\\lambda$ (algebraic multiplicity $2$).\n\nIf $A$ has *two independent eigenvectors*, then $A$ is the scalar matrix\n![](images/69/69199c6f84ebe1f9587b9207a3f1fc42c909fc9a1e43041e11fcab9ce881e5d1.svg)\nIn this case, every direction through the origin behaves like an eigenvector direction, and trajectories are straight lines:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=e^{\\lambda t} \\mathbf{v}, \\qquad \\mathbf{v} \\in \\mathbb{R}^2.\n$$\n\nSo the phase portrait is a **star node**:\n\n- If $\\lambda>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the origin and we have an **unstable star node**.\n\n- If $\\lambda<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the origin and we have a **stable star node**.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "8fe5f0d1-38d4-5cd2-98f8-8935d82677de" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14569 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "b7f1536f-3909-5575-8eee-90d0181e7613" :step/math-academy-id 588332 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167861}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167861 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168005 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch the phase portrait of the following linear system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=2x(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=2y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting our system in matrix form, we have\n![](images/f5/f58fb57caaba8c5f6a110c72565c24803ac233f41b8eb2db0c5221bcf12d7fe2.svg)\nwhere ![](images/65/65298928a44147868c82da753a731e35c3aa0ad5e4b3400819d9d9f16f546a34.svg)\n\nSince our system is decoupled, its eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=2$ with the corresponding eigenvectors $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ![](images/69/694a89326f61bbbba7e91db5dcb607a74f99881ce16059450dbe1bbac7ee797e.svg)\n\nThe general solution of the system is\n![](images/ab/abd4dbe3156abbcd645e670fb893f93cc8765cba4b1da9bde11075e211f1eeaa.svg)\n\nNotice that for each vector ![](images/9d/9df6cb20bf3163f53850730242138ec62412ccc8af23098130d71228cac4b82a.svg) we get the straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/71/7166c456c7912bf31d4ff2d98f4e4206537f8d01aca086fdba3c15b394db4b81.svg)\nthat represent the line passing through the origin and parallel to the vector ![](images/23/23074d10911b113811cc92bdc88a61a6758dbd8772c21425159c5e95d30b1075.svg)\n\nSince $e^{2t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move away from the origin as $t \\to \\infty,$ so the origin is an unstable node.\n\nThe phase portrait satisfying these conditions is the following:\n\n![](images/b3/b32b5666c0138f5c17ff1622cef9bca6fb019375f6748b6422c11a547cfad282.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ae2e5f22-ed77-5781-b5af-ab6ead3ca6d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14467 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example of a Star Node"} :step/id #uuid "b1d8e327-701e-5016-9351-b19e915b0311" :step/math-academy-id 588333 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167862}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167862 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166919 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166545 :question/id #uuid "a38dad3a-1afd-5e97-9046-87e413574865" :question/math-academy-id "e-21642"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "371d99ed-a060-51da-8f0f-7b38a0267bb7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048395}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167237} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308423} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308424} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308431} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308433} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391187} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391194} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425336}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a Decoupled System With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "470270ef-3713-5eab-bed1-4ac8b8cff293" :step/math-academy-id 588334 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167863}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167863 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168024 :tutorial/content "Recall that in this topic, we're studying phase portraits of linear systems\n![](images/28/28d8900ae13325f715af9679fdeab6f26645e322ea0df016618826a5b3dee328.svg)\nwhere the $2 \\times 2$ matrix $A$ has a *repeated* real eigenvalue $\\lambda$ (algebraic multiplicity $2$).\n\nIf $A$ has *only one eigenvector* $\\mathbf{v},$ and if $\\mathbf{w}$ is a generalized eigenvector satisfying\n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}=\\mathbf{v},\n$$\n\nthen the general solution takes the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t)=c_1 e^{\\lambda t}\\mathbf{v}+c_2 e^{\\lambda t}\\bigl(t\\,\\mathbf{v}+\\mathbf{w}\\bigr).\n$$\n\nHere, the eigenvector line (the line through the origin parallel to $\\mathbf{v}$) is still a straight-line solution, but other trajectories are *curved* and become tangent to that eigenvector line.\n\nIn cases like this, the phase portrait is a **degenerate node**:\n\n- If $\\lambda>0,$ trajectories move *away* from the origin and we have an **unstable degenerate node**.\n\n- If $\\lambda<0,$ trajectories move *toward* the origin and we have a **stable degenerate node**.\n\nLet's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f7b031de-59cf-53fe-be90-ac3711677246" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14570 :tutorial/title "Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "75bd8867-e725-5442-988b-67f110d201f4" :step/math-academy-id 588337 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167864}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167864 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186168006 :tutorial/content "Consider the following system of linear differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=x(t)+y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=y(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvector corresponding to the only eigenvalue of the system's matrix $A$ is ![](images/23/23616d197363a3fd8ad4689a0b1e547c7b1044e0929cfd96add65a26e940731f.svg) The vector ![](images/3e/3e434cc0b5ca5d288695abb58500e8f4ca6be0c0cf2ecb5200d03f5fbcf522c2.svg) is such that $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$ \n\nLet's sketch the phase portrait of the system.\n\nSince we have a repeated eigenvalue $\\lambda=1$ (of multiplicity two) and only one corresponding eigenvector $\\mathbf{v},$ the solution of the system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1\\mathbf{v} e^{\\lambda t}  + c_2 (\\mathbf{v}  te^{\\lambda t}  +  \\mathbf{w}e^{\\lambda t} \\, ),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{w}$ is a solution to the equation $(A - \\lambda I)\\mathbf{w}= \\mathbf{v}.$\n\nIn our case, \n![](images/0f/0f7c56980abff1ad84ca69aa98730bca2988cf5430f181f69d191f1e3c28eff0.svg) \nThus, the general solution of the system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}] & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]t{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}{e}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}t \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe straight-line solutions of the form\n![](images/c9/c9d65d3792c7cced40b86834fbd66769daa714ed58ffcde025cb956763c2c670.svg)\nlie on the $x$-axis. Since $e^{t} \\to \\infty$ as $t \\to \\infty,$ these solutions move toward infinity in the phase plane as $t \\to \\infty.$\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/98/98fc60d566b520f3d943e42ee8f4e07aa0bdd33b53fbb04981c2e8f4001210fd.png)\n\nNow, consider solutions of the form\n![](images/61/6170eec4537e26afef2a794426b4f5231b0f4366c21b8860c332f1ec7573b96a.svg)\nWhen $c_2=1,$ we get the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=t{e}^{t} \\\\ y={e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $1.2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-0.30$ | $0$ | $3.98$ |\n| $y$ | $0.61$ | $1$ | $3.32$ |\n\nLet's add the solutions to the phase space.\n\n![](images/4f/4fbc1650377d580d7129701d745dc8cad89d2d1d061b8f6dca8127f0ce062dc8.png)\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](images/49/499391b5bc015bd96bbe85d72e3937e87a5e89081951aaad0a752582cb87f15f.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "bfa66c2a-6bb2-552c-b495-e33f1eabed8c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14468 :tutorial/title "A Worked Example for a System With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "459bdc90-2dce-52cb-8f50-0ae602e390e4" :step/math-academy-id 588338 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167865}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167865 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166920 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166546 :question/id #uuid "e3e083fd-2421-5f66-acb5-69e1523e7fb0" :question/math-academy-id "e-21643"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2c335ee-467a-5114-a7d9-4c2692562d9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050450}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167220} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308469} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308471} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425337}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Phase Portrait of a System With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "4664ec49-c2f9-5bce-aa8d-00c4ee9589e6" :step/math-academy-id 588339 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167866}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167866 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166921 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166547 :question/id #uuid "4aa5583f-003b-55a7-bfac-ae42ac44a380" :question/math-academy-id "e-21645"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7aa8246d-6476-5574-a9d2-6133bcd169f7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048990}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167221} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167240} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186308477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391219} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186391257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186425338}] :knowledge-point/title "Classifying Systems of Linear Differential Equations With Repeated Eigenvalues"} :step/id #uuid "eb9d67cc-acaf-5e2e-a509-7e2eb99610dd" :step/math-academy-id 588342}] :activity/title "Phase Portraits for Linear Systems With Repeated Eigenvalues" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186167011}]}
